---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc970.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. Preacquisition Costs — Capitalization Criteria" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Land Development and Construction Costs" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Capitalization of Interest and Taxes During Development" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Selling Costs — Capitalizable Only If Directly Related and Recoverable" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Sales of Real Estate — Now Governed by ASC 606" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Cost Allocation for Multiple-Unit Real Estate Projects" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — Preacquisition Cost Capitalization" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Worked Example — Relative Sales Value Cost Allocation" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. ASC 970 vs IFRS — Comparison" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Related Topics" },
        { id: "s16", label: "16. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Matching development-phase costs to the projects and units they benefit</div><div class="principle">Real estate development involves a long sequence of costs — PREACQUISITION, land DEVELOPMENT, CONSTRUCTION, and eventual SELLING costs — often spanning MULTIPLE individual units or phases within a single project. ASC 970 addresses WHEN these costs should be CAPITALISED versus expensed, and HOW capitalised costs should be ALLOCATED across individual units when a project is sold off in pieces (Section 8).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Real estate developers and general real estate cost accounting</div><div class="principle">ASC 970 applies to entities involved in the DEVELOPMENT, CONSTRUCTION, and SALE of real estate projects, providing general guidance later supplemented by more industry-specific real estate Topics (CIRAs, REITs, retail land, timeshares).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Preacquisition Costs — Capitalization Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Capitalised only if directly identifiable with a specific, probable project</div><div class="principle">Costs incurred BEFORE acquiring a specific parcel of real estate (e.g. an option payment, feasibility study, or environmental assessment) are CAPITALISED only if they are DIRECTLY IDENTIFIABLE with a SPECIFIC property, would otherwise be CAPITALISED if the property were already owned, and ACQUISITION of the property (or another suitable option) is deemed PROBABLE — otherwise, such costs are EXPENSED as incurred, reflecting genuine uncertainty about whether the eventual project will proceed.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Land Development and Construction Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Direct costs building toward a saleable or rentable asset</div><div class="principle">Costs of LAND DEVELOPMENT (grading, utilities, roads) and CONSTRUCTION are CAPITALISED as part of the real estate project's cost, consistent with general asset capitalisation principles — accumulated until the project (or individual units within it) are complete and ready for sale or occupancy.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Capitalization of Interest and Taxes During Development</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Applying ASC 835's general interest capitalization framework</div><div class="principle">Real estate under DEVELOPMENT/construction is a classic "QUALIFYING ASSET" under ASC 835 (see the companion poster) — interest cost incurred during the development period is CAPITALISED as part of the project's cost, rather than expensed. Property TAXES and INSURANCE costs incurred during the development period are SIMILARLY capitalised (rather than expensed), reflecting that these are genuine costs of bringing the project to a saleable/rentable condition.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Selling Costs — Capitalizable Only If Directly Related and Recoverable</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A narrow capitalization window, tied to specific sales</div><div class="principle">Costs of SELLING real estate (e.g. sales commissions, model unit costs) are CAPITALISED only if they are DIRECTLY related to, and their recovery is REASONABLY expected from, the sale of the specific real estate — otherwise, general marketing and advertising-type selling costs are EXPENSED as incurred, consistent with GENERAL cost-of-obtaining-a-contract principles (ASC 340-40).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Sales of Real Estate — Now Governed by ASC 606</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Legacy industry-specific sale recognition rules replaced by the general control-transfer model</div><div class="principle">Revenue from SALES of real estate is now recognised under ASC 606's GENERAL control-transfer model — a genuine SIMPLIFICATION compared to legacy, real-estate-industry-SPECIFIC recognition rules that PREVIOUSLY existed (with their own bright-line down payment percentages and continuing involvement tests). Under CURRENT guidance, real estate sale recognition is analysed like any other ASC 606 contract — assessing when CONTROL of the property transfers to the buyer, and evaluating COLLECTIBILITY as part of the general contract existence criteria, WITHOUT industry-specific bright-line thresholds.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Cost Allocation for Multiple-Unit Real Estate Projects</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">The relative sales value method</div><div class="principle">When a SINGLE real estate project (e.g. a subdivision or condominium building) contains MULTIPLE individual units sold at DIFFERENT times and prices, total project costs are typically ALLOCATED to individual units using the "RELATIVE SALES VALUE" method — allocating cost in proportion to each unit's estimated relative SALES value compared to the total project's estimated sales value, rather than allocating costs equally per unit or based on square footage alone.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Preacquisition Cost Capitalization</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Capitalising costs tied to a probable acquisition</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A feasibility study before purchasing land</div><div class="wk-body">A developer incurs $150,000 for a feasibility study and environmental assessment on a specific parcel it has under a purchase OPTION, with acquisition deemed PROBABLE given advanced negotiations and secured financing commitments.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the costs are DIRECTLY identifiable with this SPECIFIC parcel and acquisition is deemed PROBABLE, the $150,000 is CAPITALISED as a preacquisition cost — to be included in the project's total cost basis once the acquisition closes.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Relative Sales Value Cost Allocation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Allocating shared project costs proportionately</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A 10-unit condominium project</div><div class="wk-body">A developer incurs $8,000,000 of total project costs for a 10-unit condominium building. Unit A has an estimated sales value of $600,000, while the TOTAL estimated sales value of all 10 units combined is $6,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Unit A's allocated cost = $8,000,000 × ($600,000 ÷ $6,000,000) = $8,000,000 × 10% = <strong>$800,000</strong> — Unit A is allocated a PROPORTIONATE share of total project costs based on its relative SALES VALUE, rather than an equal per-unit share.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 970 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Capitalisation policies for preacquisition, development, and selling costs</span><span>Interest and taxes capitalised during the development period</span><span>Cost allocation methodology used for multiple-unit projects</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether acquisition of a specific property is probable, supporting preacquisition cost capitalisation</span><span>Whether selling costs are directly related to and recoverable from a specific sale</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Relative sales values of individual units within a multi-unit project</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 970 vs IFRS — Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 970 vs general IFRS principles</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 970)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (General Principles)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Real estate sale revenue recognition</span><span>ASC 606 control transfer</span><span>IFRS 15 control transfer — converged, since legacy industry-specific rules were replaced on both sides</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Interest capitalisation during development</span><span>ASC 835 qualifying asset framework</span><span>IAS 23 borrowing costs — broadly converged core principle</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Relative sales value cost allocation</span><span>Common, described method</span><span>No dedicated named method, but general cost allocation principles achieve similar outcomes</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Real estate sale revenue recognition is one of the more thoroughly CONVERGED industry areas, given that BOTH frameworks moved from older, real-estate-specific bright-line rules to the SAME general control-transfer model.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 970 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Capitalised development costs</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Accumulated as project cost, allocated to units upon sale</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Real estate sale</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Revenue and cost of sales recognised upon control transfer</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 835</span><span>Supplies the interest capitalisation framework applied during real estate development</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the control-transfer revenue recognition model for real estate sales</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 976 / 978</span><span>Retail Land Sales and Time-Sharing Activities — related, more specialised real estate sale Topics building on this general framework</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Preacquisition costs</div><div class="glossary-def">Costs incurred before acquiring a specific property, capitalised only if directly identifiable with the property and acquisition is probable.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Relative sales value method</div><div class="glossary-def">A cost allocation method assigning shared project costs to individual units in proportion to their estimated relative sales values.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc970 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
